Set — no duplicates.

A set models a group where each element appears at most once. It answers "is this in the group?", not "what's at position three?" — there are no indexes. Add the same value twice, and the second add is simply ignored. Whenever you find yourself writing "the unique something", or checking membership, you want a set.

Here's the defining behaviour. You make a hash-set of tags, add "java", add "jvm", and then add "java" again — but that second one is ignored, because it's already present, so the size is two. Contains of "jvm" is true — and that membership check is exactly what a set is for, and it's fast. There's also a nice detail: add returns a boolean. It's true if the element was new, and false if it was already there. That's a neat way to detect first-seen values in a single step.

Now the crucial point: how does a set know two elements are the same? It decides duplicates using equals and hash code — the exact contract from modules two and three. Two elements are the same if they're equal, and a correct set requires their hash codes to match. So a hash-set of points deduplicates correctly only if point overrides equals and hash code — which is yet another reason value types should be records. Put objects with the default, identity-based equals into a hash-set, and nothing ever looks like a duplicate, even two objects with identical fields.

There are three implementations, and they differ in the iteration order they give you. Hash-set is the default, with no order at all — don't rely on its ordering. It's backed by a hash table, so add, remove, and contains are constant time on average. Reach for it unless you need ordering. Linked-hash-set keeps insertion order while iterating, at a small memory cost — use it when you want "unique, but remember the order I saw them." And tree-set keeps elements in sorted order, either natural order or by a comparator. Its operations are order log-n, not constant time, because it's a balanced tree. But it adds range queries through the navigable-set interface — first, last, ceiling of x, floor of x, head-set, and tail-set. One subtlety: a tree-set uses compare-to, or a comparator, to decide duplicates — not equals — so its ordering must be consistent with equality. More on that in section nine.

Because a set is a mathematical set, the bulk methods give you union, intersection, and difference. Add-all makes a set become the union with another. Retain-all makes it become the intersection. And remove-all makes it become the difference.

So when do you reach for a set? For membership and de-duplication: unique visitor ids, the set of permissions a user has, "have I processed this key already?", or removing duplicates from a list by wrapping it in a new hash-set. Choose hash-set by default, linked-hash-set when order-seen matters, and tree-set when you need sorted or range access. And for enum elements specifically, remember enum-set from module three — which is faster still. Next up: the map, which is really a set of keys, each carrying a value.
